1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,992
Two clerks book berths for the same
evening tide window. Clerk A counts free
slots in quay 7 and sees three. Clerk B, a
moment later, counts the same range and
also sees three. Both assign.

2
00:00:14,112 --> 00:00:26,496
The schedule board briefly shows four
vessels on three berths. Nobody rolled
back. Atomicity held inside each
transaction. Concurrent visibility did
not.

3
00:00:26,615 --> 00:00:35,807
Isolation is the contract for what
concurrent readers and writers are allowed
to observe of each other. Name the classic
anomalies in berth language.

4
00:00:35,927 --> 00:00:41,351
A dirty read would see another clerk’s
uncommitted assignment — one that might
still vanish.

5
00:00:41,471 --> 00:00:50,639
A non-repeatable read means you re-read
the same berth row inside one transaction
and get a different committed value
because someone else finished an update
between your reads.

6
00:00:50,759 --> 00:00:59,495
A phantom read is the quay-7 story: a
range query returns a new row the second
time because another transaction inserted
into that range and committed.

7
00:00:59,615 --> 00:01:11,039
Phantoms are how “three free berths”
becomes a lie without any single row
looking corrupted. Isolation levels are
the dials that permit or forbid those
anomalies, trading correctness for
throughput.

8
00:01:11,159 --> 00:01:26,783
Look at this java for a moment. Look at
this java for a moment. Spring exposes the
JDBC vocabulary on @Transactional.
READ_UNCOMMITTED allows dirty reads —
almost never what harbor scheduling wants.

9
00:01:26,903 --> 00:01:38,087
READ_COMMITTED blocks dirty reads; each
statement sees committed data, but two
reads of the same row inside one
transaction can disagree. Many engines
default here.

10
00:01:38,207 --> 00:01:46,512
REPEATABLE_READ keeps rows you already
read stable for the rest of the
transaction; phantoms may still appear
depending on the database.

11
00:01:46,632 --> 00:01:57,504
SERIALIZABLE aims at one-after-another
behavior, paid for with locks, retries, or
aborted transactions under contention.
Spring does not invent these guarantees.

12
00:01:57,624 --> 00:02:05,520
It asks the PlatformTransactionManager to
set isolation on the connection when the
transaction begins — if the driver and
engine honor it.

13
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:17,856
PostgreSQL, MySQL, and Oracle do not
implement every textbook promise the same
way. Raising isolation on a hot berth path
without reading your engine’s docs is
optimism, not safety.

14
00:02:17,975 --> 00:02:28,752
Why declare isolation on the method at
all? Because the connection pool default
may be fine for a quay board read and too
weak for an assignment that must not
invent phantom capacity.

15
00:02:28,871 --> 00:02:37,727
Putting the level next to the boundary
documents intent for the next engineer who
touches concurrent booking. Misconceptions
cluster here.

16
00:02:37,847 --> 00:02:51,431
People assume REPEATABLE_READ alone
prevents lost updates without locking or
version checks — isolation addresses read
phenomena; lost updates still need careful
update patterns, @Version, or SELECT … FOR
UPDATE.

17
00:02:51,551 --> 00:03:04,151
Others set everything to SERIALIZABLE “to
be safe,” then drown in serialization
failures. Another trap: believing the
annotation alone fixes two requests that
each read “three free” before either
writes.

18
00:03:04,271 --> 00:03:16,919
You still need constraints or explicit
locks. Isolation is one tool, not the
whole kit. We can commit cleanly and still
be wrong under concurrency unless we name
the visibility contract.

19
00:03:17,039 --> 00:03:26,087
The next knob is different: which
exceptions should undo the work when a
business rule fails as a checked type.
Rollback rules encode that policy.
